     NetHack hasn't used file timestamps for a long time.
It used to throw away save files which appeared to be
older than the program itself, on the assumption that if
the program was newer it might have been modified then
rebuilt and no longer be compatible with the saved data.
This lead to problems for systems which had unreliable
clocks, and was replaced by storing version/sanity data
in the save file and checking that against the current
program configuration when restoring the saved game.

     Data corruption could be mistakenly treated as a
trickery attempt.  There's also a compile-time config
option that is supposed to help workstations in an NFS
environment avoid spurious trickery complaints, but I'm
not sure what it actually does.  I don't recall offhand
what it's called, and it may be set up in unixconf.h
rather than in config.h.


